$ These are the sets of functions selected from the TCA archive (apart from SCF):
$  ['correlating']
$ 
$ dyall.7p.dz.bas
$
$ el contained in the file 
$ Uut Uuq Uup Uus Uuo Uuh
$ 
$ 7p Block Double-Zeta Basis Set Archive
$ ======================================
$ 
$ 
$ Date: 18 Nov 2009
$ 
$ 
$ When publishing work that uses these basis sets, please use the following citation:
$ 
$ K.G. Dyall, in preparation. Basis sets are available from the Dirac web site, 
$ http://dirac.chem.sdu.dk.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ This archive contains the exponents and the SCF spinor or orbital coefficients, 
$ given with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
$ the double-zeta basis sets described in the above articles.  
$ 
$ NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the largest. 
$ 
$ The SCF exponent sets were generated as follows:
$ 
$ * Optimization of the s, p, d, and f sets separately, on the total energy of the
$   configuration average of the s^2 p^n configurationn, to yield a 26s 23p 16d 10f
$   set.
$ * Addition of the valence correlating d exponent to the d set and reoptimization of the
$   other 16 d exponents, with the correlating d exponent fixed
$ * Replacement of the outermost SCF f exponent with the 5d correlating f exponent, and
$   reoptimization of the other 9 f exponents with the correlating f exponent fixed.
$ 
$ The resulting primitive basis set is a 26s 23p 17d 10f set. In all the following
$ descriptions, this is the set referred to as the SCF exponent set. 
$ 
$ The correlating exponents were generated as follows
$ 
$ * A d exponent for 7s7p correlation was optimized in MR-SDCI calculations on the
$   ground configuration, with the outermost s exponent and p exponent from the
$   SCF set included in the correlating space and held fixed. (These are the optimal
$   s and p correlating exponents from the SCF set.)
$ * An f exponent for 6d correlation was optimized in MR-SDCI calculations on the
$   ground configuration, with double excitations from the 5d shell into the f
$   shell coupled to J=0.
$ 
$ The diffuse exponent sets were generated as follows.
$ 
$ * An s exponent and a p exponent were optimized in SCF calculations on the
$   negative ion for Uut - Uus, with the remaining s and p exponents fixed
$ * A d exponent was optimized in MR-SDCI calculations on the negative ion 
$   for Uut - Uus, with the correlating s, p, and d set and the diffuse sp set
$   included in the correlating space, i.e. a 2s2p2d set.
$ * For Uuo, a diffuse s, p, and d exponent was optimized for dipole polarization
$   of the 7p shell, with 7p -> s and d excitations in the perturbing space for 
$   the s and d exponents, and 7s -> p excitations in the perturbing space for 
$   the p exponents.
$ 
$ The exponents for 6d dipole polarization were generated as follows:
$ 
$ * A polarizing f exponent was optimized on the polarizability of the 6d shell
$   with 6d -> f excitations in the perturbing space. 
$ 
$ 
$ The archive is set out as follows.
$ 
$ 1. Exponents and SCF coefficients for Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions, in
$    order of Z. The large component functions are given first, followed by the
$    small component functions. Both components are labelled with the large
$    component symmetry type, but the primitive functions for the small component
$    are those obtained by restricted kinetic balance from the large component.  
$    The spin-orbit components are labeled with "-" for j=l-1/2 and "+" for j=l+1/2.   
$ 
$ 2. Exponents and SCF coefficients for spin-free modified Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave
$    functions, in order of Z. The large component functions are given first, 
$    followed by the pseudo-large component functions, then the Foldy-Wouthuysen
$    transformed large component functions. Note that all components have the same
$    symmetry type. 
$ 
$ 3. Exponents of diffuse s, p, and d functions.
$ 
$ 4. Exponents of valence and core correlating functions.
$ 
$ 5. Exponents of core polarizing functions.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ Basis set construction.
$ ----------------------
$ 
$ Several different types of basis sets may be constructed by adding primitive
$ functions. These basis sets are described below.
$ 
$ NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the largest. 
$ 
$ 1. Valence (equivalent to cc-pvdz). To the SCF occupied set, add the following:
$    1st s primitive from the SCF set 
$    1st p primitive from the SCF set
$    1st d primitive from the SCF set (correlating d)
$ 
$    (HF + 1s1p1d valence)
$ 
$ 2. Core-valence. To basis set 1, add the following:
$    3rd s primitive from the SCF set 
$    3rd p primitive from the SCF set
$    2nd d primitive from the SCF set 
$    1st f primitive from the SCF set (correlating f)
$ 
$    (HF + 1s1p1d valence + 1s1p1d1f core)
$ 
$ 3. Dipole polarization of the (n-1)d shell. To basis set 2, add the polarizing
$    f exponent.
$ 
$    (HF + 1s1p1d valence + 1s1p1d2f core)
$ 
$ 4. Diffuse basis: add the diffuse s, p and d functions to any of the above
$    basis sets.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ 

